---

<ArticleLayout
	title="When a World Starts to Take Shape — Fhugawz"
	description={excerpt}
	spanishTitle="Cuando un Mundo empieza a tomar forma — Fhugawz"
	spanishDescription="Notas sobre cómo la identidad visual de cada Mundo me ayuda a entender la música, la secuencia y lo que realmente pertenece dentro de cada parte de Fhugawz."
	keywords={keywords}
	type="article"
	image={heroImage}
	imageAlt="Fhugawz observing a nocturnal scene while developing the visual identity of the Worlds."
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroKicker="PROJECT DIARY"
	heroLede={excerpt}
>
	<div class="article-world-shape">
		<p>I’ve been comparing several of the Fhugawz covers and realized something that probably should have been obvious, but I had never really thought about it this way.</p>
		<p>There are Worlds I started understanding visually before I completely understood them through the music.</p>
		<p>That does not mean the image always comes first. Sometimes I have a fairly clear sense of the songs and still have no idea what the record should look like. Other times the opposite happens: I find an image, a texture or a particular way of lighting something and suddenly I understand the music a little better.</p>
		<p>I think that is why it is becoming harder for me to separate the two.</p>

		<h2>I am not trying to make everything look the same</h2>
		<p>It is very easy to confuse coherence with repetition.</p>
		<p>If you have a certain palette, a typeface, a photographic treatment and keep repeating them, everything quickly starts looking like it belongs to the same project.</p>
		<p>The problem is that Fhugawz does not really work that way.</p>
		<p>If the thirteen Worlds represent different states, it would not make much sense for all of them to look like variations of the same cover. 199X should not look like Phobia, just as Phobia should not feel visually like 314.</p>
		<p>I am trying to let each one develop its own language.</p>
		<p>Sometimes that means changing the photography completely, working with a different amount of detail, removing color, bringing it back, making something much cleaner or going in almost the opposite direction.</p>
		<p>The difficult part is understanding how far I can move without losing the connection between them.</p>
		<p>I am still figuring out where that limit is.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src={`${imageBase}/ai-assisted-visual-assets-music-world-building.webp`} alt="A rainy nocturnal scene showing one possible visual language within the Fhugawz Worlds." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>An image can tell me I still do not understand the record</h2>
		<p>This has happened several times.</p>
		<p>I have a visual idea that I really like on its own. I work on it, look at it for a while and technically there is nothing particularly wrong with it.</p>
		<p>But it does not belong.</p>
		<p>Before, I probably would have tried to justify it because I had already spent time working on it. Now I try to pay more attention to that feeling.</p>
		<p>If I have to explain too much why an image belongs to a particular World, there is probably something I still do not understand.</p>
		<p>And sometimes the problem is not even the image.</p>
		<p>I go back to the songs and realize I was not completely sure what I was trying to represent there either.</p>
		<p>That is one of the things I like about working visually. Sometimes it reveals a problem that actually started somewhere else.</p>

		<h2>The covers are becoming reference points</h2>
		<p>Now that several covers already exist together, I can look at the sequence differently.</p>
		<p>Not only as thirteen individual designs.</p>
		<p>I can put them next to each other and see whether I actually feel an evolution.</p>
		<p>Things appear and disappear. The character changes age, the environments change, and so does the distance from which we observe everything. Some images are much more intimate while others begin to feel enormous or simply strange.</p>
		<p>That helps me quite a lot.</p>
		<p>A cover can work perfectly well by itself and still interrupt the sequence once I place it next to everything else.</p>
		<p>That has already happened.</p>
		<p>It will probably happen again.</p>
		<p>I would rather notice those problems while the project is still alive than after everything is supposedly finished.</p>

		<h2>I do not want “dark” to become a formula</h2>
		<p>For a long time it would have been very easy to describe Fhugawz visually as simply dark.</p>
		<p>There is still darkness throughout a lot of the project.</p>
		<p>But that word is starting to feel too small.</p>
		<p>Dark can very quickly become black, fog, grain, low light and someone looking down. If I repeat that thirteen times, it stops meaning much.</p>
		<p>I am more interested in understanding why an image needs to feel dark.</p>
		<p>In 199X it can come from memory. In Fake/minds it can feel more physical, youthful and aggressive. In Protocol 7 the darkness starts relating to another kind of isolation, while later Worlds can push it toward something uncomfortable or surreal.</p>
		<p>Some traces can remain across them.</p>
		<p>The reason behind those traces does not have to be the same.</p>
		<p>I think that distinction matters.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src={`${imageBase}/fhugawz-314-creative-resources-visual-worlds.webp`} alt="A green visual language associated with 314 and the changing identity of the later Worlds." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>I am also learning to leave things out</h2>
		<p>Another thing I have noticed is that I tend to want too many ideas inside one image.</p>
		<p>If a World contains several concepts, my first instinct is often to represent all of them.</p>
		<p>Usually that does not work.</p>
		<p>I am becoming more interested in letting a cover hold one strong feeling and allowing the songs to do the rest.</p>
		<p>I do not need to fit the entire story inside a square.</p>
		<p>Sometimes a silhouette, a particular distance between two characters or an empty space is enough.</p>
		<p>And if someone listens to the record later and begins to see that image differently because of what they heard, even better.</p>
		<p>I prefer that to explaining everything visually from the beginning.</p>

		<h2>I think I am looking for the same thing from two directions</h2>
		<p>When I work on the songs, I am trying to understand what belongs to each World.</p>
		<p>With the images I am doing almost the same thing.</p>
		<p>Sometimes I arrive through sound and other times an image shows me something I had not yet found musically.</p>
		<p>That is why the covers no longer feel like something I will solve after the records are finished.</p>
		<p>They are part of how I am understanding them while I build them.</p>
		<p>And several will probably change before reaching their final versions.</p>
		<p>That does not bother me.</p>
		<p>If I look at a cover a few months from now and feel that the World no longer lives there, I would rather change it.</p>
		<p>In the end I am not trying to create thirteen memorable images in isolation.</p>
		<p>I am trying to reach a point where, when all thirteen are together, I can see the same journey I am trying to hear.</p>
	</div>
</ArticleLayout>
